[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`driver` with an over-long `len` aborted instead of faulting.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Found by the `len == INT_MAX` case of `boundary_sweep_len_domain_faulting`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    The C `int out[len]` is a stack VLA, so a `len` whose array cannot fit in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    remaining stack dies with `SIGSEGV` (signal 11). The translation used
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `vec![0; len]`, so the same input hit a *heap* allocation failure and Rust's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    allocation-error handler aborted with `SIGABRT` (signal 6), after printing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `memory allocation of 8589934588 bytes failed`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Fix: `driver` now calls `vla_stack_probe(n_bytes)` before allocating. It
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    reads the current thread's stack extent (`pthread_getattr_np` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `pthread_attr_getstack`) and, when the array would not fit, performs the same
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    below-the-stack access the C `memcpy` performs, producing an identical
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `SIGSEGV`. Sizes under 64 KiB and byte counts that wrap the address space
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    (negative `len`) skip the probe, because the C faults inside `memcpy` in those
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    cases and the translation already does too (rows 17-18).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **`fma_array` with a NULL pointer aborted instead of faulting — in debug
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    builds only.** Found by running rows 6-9 against the *debug* `.so` as well as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the release one. With `debug-assertions` on, rustc inserts a UB precondition
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    check on raw-pointer dereferences, so the null deref panicked and, because
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    unwinding cannot cross `extern "C"`, aborted with `SIGABRT` (6) where the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    dies with `SIGSEGV` (11). The release `.so` had no such check and matched.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Fix: `debug-assertions` and `overflow-checks` are disabled for **both**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    profiles in `Cargo.toml`, since the C reference performs no null, alignment
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    or overflow checking; the loop counters were also switched to explicit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `wrapping_add` so the source does not depend on that profile setting.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
